<|channel>thought
<channel|>We spend hours architecting complex personal knowledge bases, obsessing over graph views and nested tags. But we are overlooking a massive blind spot: accessibility. If your "second brain" isn't navigable via screen readers or keyboard-only shortcuts, it isn't a tool—it's a barrier. We need to stop designing for aesthetics alone and start building for usability. Our digital gardens must be universal, ensuring our knowledge remains accessible and functional for everyone, regardless of how they interface with their devices. [end of text]


